    {
      "@id": "x:n66",
      "@type": [
        "x:_schema_Protocol",
        "Protocol"
      ],
      "title": "On each trial, present a safe option and a lottery with two equiprobable outcomes in one of three conditions (Solo: participant chooses for self; Social: participant chooses for self and partner; Par…",
      "content": "On each trial, present a safe option and a lottery with two equiprobable outcomes in one of three conditions (Solo: participant chooses for self; Social: participant chooses for self and partner; Partner: partner chooses for both); when the lottery is chosen in Social or Partner trials, play it out independently for each player; simulate partner choices with an algorithm that selects the option with the highest expected value.\n\nPer session 20 mixed, 20 gain and 20 loss trials. Mixed trials: safe 0 € vs lottery with gain from [15, 25, 40, 55, 75] cents and loss equal to the gain times one of [-0.2, –0.34, −0.5, –0.64, −0.77, –0.89, −1, –1.1, −1.35, –2]. Gain trials: safe gain from [10, 15, 20, 25, 30] cents vs lottery of 0 € or the safe amount times one of [1.68, 1.82, 2, 2.22, 2.48, 2.8, 3.16, 3.6, 4.2, 5]. Loss trials: certain loss from [–10, –15, −20, –25, –30] cents vs lottery of 0 € or a larger loss using the same multipliers. Option positions randomized; trial types randomized with no more than two of the same condition in a row; unlimited decision time; decisions shown 2 s, outcomes 2.5 s; ISI 1–2 s from a gamma distribution (3–11 s in Study 2). Implemented in MATLAB with Psychtoolbox.",
      "created": "2026-10-16T13:00:37.200Z",
      "modified": "2026-10-16T13:00:37.200Z",
      "creator": "x:agent",
      "description": "x:a-n66"
    },
    {
      "@id": "x:n67",
      "@type": [
        "x:_schema_Protocol",
        "Protocol"
      ],
      "title": "Before the task, have participant and partner play a 15-minute icebreaker game in which each takes turns drawing one half of a simple picture while blindfolded, following verbal instructions and enco…",
      "content": "Before the task, have participant and partner play a 15-minute icebreaker game in which each takes turns drawing one half of a simple picture while blindfolded, following verbal instructions and encouragement from the other.\n\nAfterwards participants rated the partner on sympathy, cooperativity, honesty, openness and sociability on a 1–10 scale.",
      "created": "2026-10-16T13:00:37.200Z",
      "modified": "2026-10-16T13:00:37.200Z",
      "creator": "x:agent",
      "description": "x:a-n67"
    },
    {
      "@id": "x:n68",
      "@type": [
        "x:_schema_Protocol",
        "Protocol"
      ],
      "title": "Every two trials, one ISI after the previous outcome, ask 'How happy are you right now?' and record a response on a 100-option scale from 'very unhappy' to 'very happy' by moving a cursor starting at…",
      "content": "Every two trials, one ISI after the previous outcome, ask 'How happy are you right now?' and record a response on a 100-option scale from 'very unhappy' to 'very happy' by moving a cursor starting at the midpoint; Z-score ratings within participant for analysis.",
      "created": "2026-10-16T13:00:37.200Z",
      "modified": "2026-10-16T13:00:37.200Z",
      "creator": "x:agent",
      "description": "x:a-n68"
    },
    {
      "@id": "x:n69",
      "@type": [
        "x:_schema_Protocol",
        "Protocol"
      ],
      "title": "Model the choice of the risky option with a mixed-effects logistic regression on the mean-centred expected-value difference (EVdiff), condition (Solo vs Social) and their interaction, with subject-sp…",
      "content": "Model the choice of the risky option with a mixed-effects logistic regression on the mean-centred expected-value difference (EVdiff), condition (Solo vs Social) and their interaction, with subject-specific random intercepts and slopes, fitted separately per study with lme4 in R (Equation 1).",
      "created": "2026-10-16T13:00:37.200Z",
      "modified": "2026-10-16T13:00:37.200Z",
      "creator": "x:agent",
      "description": "x:a-n69"
    },
    {
      "@id": "x:n70",
      "@type": [
        "x:_schema_Protocol",
        "Protocol"
      ],
      "title": "For each participant and condition, fit a logistic regression of risky choice on EVdiff, define the risk premium as the EVdiff value at which the fitted model predicts 50% risky choices, and compare …",
      "content": "For each participant and condition, fit a logistic regression of risky choice on EVdiff, define the risk premium as the EVdiff value at which the fitted model predicts 50% risky choices, and compare Solo and Social risk premiums with paired t-tests (Equation 2).",
      "created": "2026-10-16T13:00:37.200Z",
      "modified": "2026-10-16T13:00:37.200Z",
      "creator": "x:agent",
      "description": "x:a-n70"
    },
    {
      "@id": "x:n71",
      "@type": [
        "x:_schema_Protocol",
        "Protocol"
      ],
      "title": "Estimate a constant absolute risk aversion parameter ρ per participant and condition by fitting a probit regression with a cumulative Gaussian link, Fechner noise σ, and CARA certainty equivalents to…",
      "content": "Estimate a constant absolute risk aversion parameter ρ per participant and condition by fitting a probit regression with a cumulative Gaussian link, Fechner noise σ, and CARA certainty equivalents to choices (non-linear least squares in Matlab nlinfit.m), then compare Solo and Social ρ with paired t-tests (Equation 3).\n\nUtility U(x,ρ) = 1 − e(−ρx); expected lottery utility p·U(x1,ρ) + (1−p)·U(x2,ρ); certainty equivalent log(1 − ρ·EU)/−ρ. ρ was first estimated separately for gain and loss trials and pooled when these did not differ.",
      "created": "2026-10-16T13:00:37.200Z",
      "modified": "2026-10-16T13:00:37.200Z",
      "creator": "x:agent",
      "description": "x:a-n71"
    },
    {
      "@id": "x:n72",
      "@type": [
        "x:_schema_Protocol",
        "Protocol"
      ],
      "title": "Fit five computational models of momentary happiness to each participant's Z-scored ratings, each summing exponentially decaying (forgetting factor γ) influences of certain rewards, expected value of…",
      "content": "Fit five computational models of momentary happiness to each participant's Z-scored ratings, each summing exponentially decaying (forgetting factor γ) influences of certain rewards, expected value of chosen lotteries and participant reward prediction errors, plus model-specific terms: Inequality (|S−P|), Guilt-envy (advantageous and disadvantageous inequality separately), Responsibility (partner RPEs from participant and partner choices separately) and Responsibility Redux (partner RPEs from participant choices only); compare models by R2, adjusted R2, AIC, BIC and pairwise likelihood ratio tests (Equations 4–9).\n\nNo constant term because ratings were Z-scored; terms for unchosen options set to zero. LR = −2·(log L1 − log L0), Chi-square distributed with degrees of freedom equal to the number of extra parameters.",
      "created": "2026-10-16T13:00:37.200Z",
      "modified": "2026-10-16T13:00:37.200Z",
      "creator": "x:agent",
      "description": "x:a-n72"
    },
    {
      "@id": "x:n73",
      "@type": [
        "x:_schema_Protocol",
        "Protocol"
      ],
      "title": "Synthesize happiness data from each participant's fitted parameters, add 1 SD of noise, refit the model, repeat 10 times, and regress the mean recovered parameter on the actual parameter for each par…",
      "content": "Synthesize happiness data from each participant's fitted parameters, add 1 SD of noise, refit the model, repeat 10 times, and regress the mean recovered parameter on the actual parameter for each participant.",
      "created": "2026-10-16T13:00:37.200Z",
      "modified": "2026-10-16T13:00:37.200Z",
      "creator": "x:agent",
      "description": "x:a-n73"
    },
    {
      "@id": "x:n74",
      "@type": [
        "x:_schema_Protocol",
        "Protocol"
      ],
      "title": "Regress Z-scored happiness reported after lottery outcomes in Social and Partner trials on dummy variables for the participant receiving the high outcome, the partner receiving the high outcome, the …",
      "content": "Regress Z-scored happiness reported after lottery outcomes in Social and Partner trials on dummy variables for the participant receiving the high outcome, the partner receiving the high outcome, the participant having chosen, and their pairwise interactions, in a mixed-effects linear model with subject random intercepts fitted with lme4 (Equation 10).",
      "created": "2026-10-16T13:00:37.200Z",
      "modified": "2026-10-16T13:00:37.200Z",
      "creator": "x:agent",
      "description": "x:a-n74"
    },
    {
      "@id": "x:n75",
      "@type": [
        "x:_schema_Protocol",
        "Protocol"
      ],
      "title": "Acquire T2* EPI BOLD images on a 3T Siemens TRIO with a 32-channel head coil (TR 2500 ms, TE 30 ms, 37 slices, 2 × 2 × 3 mm voxels, flip angle 90°, FOV 192 mm, PAT 2) plus a T1 structural (TR 1660 ms…",
      "content": "Acquire T2* EPI BOLD images on a 3T Siemens TRIO with a 32-channel head coil (TR 2500 ms, TE 30 ms, 37 slices, 2 × 2 × 3 mm voxels, flip angle 90°, FOV 192 mm, PAT 2) plus a T1 structural (TR 1660 ms, 208 slices, 0.8 mm isotropic); discard the first five volumes, realign, coregister, normalize to MNI305 by segmentation, resample to 2 mm, smooth with an 8 mm FWHM Gaussian kernel and high-pass filter at 128 s in SPM12.",
      "created": "2026-10-16T13:00:37.200Z",
      "modified": "2026-10-16T13:00:37.200Z",
      "creator": "x:agent",
      "description": "x:a-n75"
    },
    {
      "@id": "x:n76",
      "@type": [
        "x:_schema_Protocol",
        "Protocol"
      ],
      "title": "Fit a fixed-effects GLM (GLM1) per participant with stick-function regressors for option presentation, safe choice, risky choice and safe-outcome receipt per condition, high and low partner lottery o…",
      "content": "Fit a fixed-effects GLM (GLM1) per participant with stick-function regressors for option presentation, safe choice, risky choice and safe-outcome receipt per condition, high and low partner lottery outcomes coded separately for Social and Partner conditions, participant high and low outcomes, happiness button presses and six motion parameters; enter parameter estimates in random-effects full factorial and paired t-test group models; report clusters at p < 0.05 FWE-corrected (whole brain or small volume) with a cluster-forming threshold of p < 0.001.",
      "created": "2026-10-16T13:00:37.200Z",
      "modified": "2026-10-16T13:00:37.200Z",
      "creator": "x:agent",
      "description": "x:a-n76"
    },
    {
      "@id": "x:n77",
      "@type": [
        "x:_schema_Protocol",
        "Protocol"
      ],
      "title": "Regress GLM1 parameter estimates from all voxels of regions of interest on factors Choice, Condition, Run and ROI (decision phase) or Social, LowOutcome and Run (outcome phase) with all interactions,…",
      "content": "Regress GLM1 parameter estimates from all voxels of regions of interest on factors Choice, Condition, Run and ROI (decision phase) or Social, LowOutcome and Run (outcome phase) with all interactions, using mixed-effects linear models with subject random intercepts fitted with fitlme.m (restricted maximum likelihood); retain the better-fitting model with or without Run by BIC and follow up with contrasts.",
      "created": "2026-10-16T13:00:37.200Z",
      "modified": "2026-10-16T13:00:37.200Z",
      "creator": "x:agent",
      "description": "x:a-n77"
    },
    {
      "@id": "x:n78",
      "@type": [
        "x:_schema_Protocol",
        "Protocol"
      ],
      "title": "Build a second GLM (GLM2) whose regressors are stick functions at event times with magnitudes given by the fitted Responsibility model's certain reward, expected value, participant RPE, social_pRPE a…",
      "content": "Build a second GLM (GLM2) whose regressors are stick functions at event times with magnitudes given by the fitted Responsibility model's certain reward, expected value, participant RPE, social_pRPE and partner_pRPE values, convolved with the canonical HRF, plus button presses and motion parameters, and test the resulting estimate maps in a random-effects model.",
      "created": "2026-10-16T13:00:37.200Z",
      "modified": "2026-10-16T13:00:37.200Z",
      "creator": "x:agent",
      "description": "x:a-n78"
    },
    {
      "@id": "x:n79",
      "@type": [
        "x:_schema_Protocol",
        "Protocol"
      ],
      "title": "Run seed-to-voxel psychophysiological interaction analyses with the gPPI toolbox, using as seeds the left insula cluster sensitive to risky versus safe outcomes (GLM3) and the left STS cluster respon…",
      "content": "Run seed-to-voxel psychophysiological interaction analyses with the gPPI toolbox, using as seeds the left insula cluster sensitive to risky versus safe outcomes (GLM3) and the left STS cluster responding more to social_pRPE than partner_pRPE (GLM4), modelling the interaction between the seed's first eigenvariate and regressors for Safe and Risky choices in Social, Partner and Solo conditions; test Choice by Condition interactions in full factorial group models.",
      "created": "2026-10-16T13:00:37.200Z",
      "modified": "2026-10-16T13:00:37.200Z",
      "creator": "x:agent",
      "description": "x:a-n79"
    },
    {
      "@id": "x:n80",
      "@type": [
        "x:_schema_Protocol",
        "Protocol"
      ],
      "title": "Compute the dot product between each participant's response map (low partner outcomes after participant versus partner choices) and the published guilt-related brain signature, test the sign of the v…",
      "content": "Compute the dot product between each participant's response map (low partner outcomes after participant versus partner choices) and the published guilt-related brain signature, test the sign of the values with a sign test, and correlate them with the behavioural guilt effect using Spearman's Rho.",
      "created": "2026-10-16T13:00:37.200Z",
      "modified": "2026-10-16T13:00:37.200Z",
      "creator": "x:agent",
      "description": "x:a-n80"
